#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce50e834-b8c1-56da-8750-241601cd1046" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "0a5038b8-1c2e-591c-9190-9493014fd98a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1506" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}1},$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac 4 1 = {\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{red}4} \\cdot a_{n} , \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}1}, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "25a92b77-314f-5e6a-8a26-5e631d10e4d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1507" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -3 a_n,\\qquad a_1 = 2,\\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-3{a}_{1}=-3(2)=-6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-3{a}_{2}=-3(-6)=18 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-3{a}_{3}=-3(18)=-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_4 = -54.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76437951-0d71-548b-b330-e494aa12c6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "33621cf4-4508-51d5-958b-18a6ec1ebc73" :question/math-academy-id "e-1508" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $10, \\: 20, \\: 30, \\: 40, \\: \\dots$\n\nII. $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n,\\quad a_1 = 36,\\quad n\\geq 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1) = 2f(n)-1,\\quad f(1) =2,\\quad  n\\geq 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{10} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{30}{20} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule $a_{n+1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}a_n$ tells us that each term is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\cdot f(1)-1=2\\cdot 2-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\cdot f(2)-1=2\\cdot 3-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)}=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae4cf92-f456-5b5f-bf92-1c4aed5ee4f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Recursive Formula in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "ae9a3613-9e84-53bd-ad35-48394adfdc30" :question/math-academy-id "e-4232" :question/problem "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -5f(n),\\quad f(1) = -1,\\quad  n\\geq 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-5f(1)=-5(-1)=5 \\\\ f(3) & =-5f(2)=-5(5)=-25 \\\\ f(4) & =-5f(3)=-5(-25)=125 \\\\ f(5) & =-5f(4)=-5(125)=-625\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(5)=-625.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce50e834-b8c1-56da-8750-241601cd1046" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009c5090-9761-5896-a02c-03e25cbe6026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{8}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548358} {:answer/id #uuid "72123f1f-d2d0-5b74-98de-1e0e9c6f018c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{45}{4}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=15,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548567} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81aff5-4139-5c1a-bd69-74881d053800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548694} {:answer/id #uuid "d73001c1-8eb9-52f1-a68d-ccd036e4b502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{20}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=15,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548773} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf82a41-3a9d-594e-8658-634533fb08a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf82a41-3a9d-594e-8658-634533fb08a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548785} :answer-field/id #uuid "8095106a-9247-5646-9b1f-f9986551763f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31eb43e4-a693-51d4-837e-e49e62a1a72a" :question/math-academy-id "q-101258" :question/problem "What is the recursive formula for the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n20,\\,15,\\,\\frac{45}{4},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=20,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15}{20}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the recursive formula for this sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae4cf92-f456-5b5f-bf92-1c4aed5ee4f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Recursive Formula in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00bac6ed-4ca1-5d37-a955-d3ac6bd3a6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548359} {:answer/id #uuid "24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548428} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ad70f6-91a3-5b4a-af2e-ce791c283024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548677} {:answer/id #uuid "c65f894c-ad2b-5fd9-88a7-4d2f848bde7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-300.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548747} {:answer/id #uuid "ec977cf1-c906-53fd-b5f5-1fb310eb6eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-85.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548428} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08de971-253f-5c62-b587-1f127bb77b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7787ac5-6aeb-5774-9055-b078c9c07d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-101432" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-3.5f(n),\\,f(1)=-2,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =-3.5f(1)=-3.5(-2)=7 \\\\ f(3) & =-3.5f(2)=-3.5(7)=-24.5 \\\\ f(4) & =-3.5f(3)=-3.5(-24.5)=85.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=85.75.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76437951-0d71-548b-b330-e494aa12c6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed8ab5a-274b-5b22-8b12-39cf14734b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548475} {:answer/id #uuid "433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548482} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe85af2-0fd8-5185-920a-010f63408182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548669} {:answer/id #uuid "ae09be8b-9fec-50e0-ab6a-459b64c04f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548699} {:answer/id #uuid "fc97ca3f-aeac-59db-8044-ae6fc8558f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548482} :answer-field/id #uuid "1136a664-65b2-52c5-a34a-dc3466e7ae31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baaafa36-2999-5769-af9b-da0e59cba7b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101443" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6},\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+{a}_{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=5f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{5},\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I could be a geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2+{2}^{2}=6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+{6}^{2}=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6}{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{42}{6} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ f(2) & =5(\\frac{1}{5})+3=4 \\\\ f(3) & =5(4)+3=23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=20 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{23}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only I is a geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76437951-0d71-548b-b330-e494aa12c6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223d1a4a-f12e-53dc-b2c2-3f53b62f9cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548423} {:answer/id #uuid "8b172897-6256-56d7-a919-3132661e3862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548622} {:answer/id #uuid "8b95b35a-939e-5d64-a7a0-741bd14bb687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548624} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbf5972-ecfe-5bfa-9e97-3f8d36df9174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548631} {:answer/id #uuid "99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548658} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a716f11-43d9-5db6-97b8-516caa9bdfb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1faddb6e-7103-543e-9a46-6187e7487263" :question/math-academy-id "q-109811" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{16}{3},\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,-20,\\,80,\\,…$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=4f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{16}{3})}{(-\\frac{4}{3})}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-20}{-5}=4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{80}{-20}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =4\\cdot 2-4=4 \\\\ f(3) & =4\\cdot 4-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, none of the given is a geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce50e834-b8c1-56da-8750-241601cd1046" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Recursive Formula for a Geometric Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9007d100-56e2-5122-835f-80d3456291ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}a_n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9007d100-56e2-5122-835f-80d3456291ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}a_n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548637} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a037a71-ae84-5e7f-b898-efcae2af52b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "224edbc2-fbee-5d40-bf96-326d59bc470b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224edbc2-fbee-5d40-bf96-326d59bc470b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548424} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa0faa4-4e5a-549c-865c-470803bc7a75" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95752b1d-23a5-5822-b360-684f0368d8b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-249790" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n30,\\,50,\\,\\frac{250}{3},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe recursive formula for this geometric sequence is\n\n${a}_{n+1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{a}_{1}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence with common ratio $r$ takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{1}=30,$ and we can calculate the common ratio $r$ as\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{50}{30}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{5}{3}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=30,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e349f683-d5d2-533c-bbf7-eb7857abb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e349f683-d5d2-533c-bbf7-eb7857abb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548796} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6223f65-65ec-56a2-89b6-bc66582ce68b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03727fdc-9b2a-53db-b355-f55f8718eb73" :question/math-academy-id "q-249803" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=27,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd term of this geometric sequence is ${a}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $3$rd term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =27 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}(27)=18 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}(18)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{3}=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027fccaa-c5ae-5f11-8896-43a5a65af33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027fccaa-c5ae-5f11-8896-43a5a65af33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548363} :answer-field/id #uuid "801cf6ff-9c43-59b4-97d9-599eaa1e94e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "385a6fb7-8690-582d-88d4-8765c8c27e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-249805" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=256,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $4$th term of this geometric sequence is ${a}_{4}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =256 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(256)=64 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}(64)=16 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}(16)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae4cf92-f456-5b5f-bf92-1c4aed5ee4f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Recursive Formula in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548608} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c7e41f-ae27-5892-83ef-b3b75e7b59b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e60274a9-ad7a-5782-a789-be03c76f6f3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-249892" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{2}{7}f(n),\\,f(1)=343,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $4$th term of this geometric sequence is $f(4)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =343 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}(343)=98 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(2)=\\frac{2}{7}(98)=28 \\\\ f(4) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(3)=\\frac{2}{7}(28)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aee1b1d-3cb6-5f52-8973-13b067b1c41a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Term of a Geometric Sequence Given Its Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53dc9885-bcbf-5231-b7b6-371ca0f2ff5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548513} {:answer/id #uuid "a173cd67-30bb-555b-b5f9-349f5d3f36c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 